-Anticoherence is characterized by isotropic low-order spin moments. For pure states, it emerges from quantum superpositions and is relevant to direction-independent metrology and quantum reference-frame alignment. For mixed states such isotropy may also originate from classical statistical mixing. To formalize this difference, we introduce here an axiomatic framework for mixed-state $t$-anticoherence in the symmetric $N=2j$ qubit representation, with $1\le t<N$. We distinguish \emph{total} $t$-anticoherence, quantifying the reduced-state isotropy, from \emph{quantum} $t$-anticoherence, defined relative to a chosen total measure, which quantifies the isotropy coming from entanglement between the $t$ and $N-t$ qubits. This yields a \emph{classical} contribution as their difference. For concave total measures, this classical contribution admits an ensemble/measurement interpretation analogous to a Koashi–Winter relation. We construct total anticoherence measures based on distances to or overlaps with the maximally mixed state and quantum counterparts via convex-roof extensions of pure-state anticoherence measures. For the trace distance and fidelity candidates, their general covariant-noise monotonicity remains open. We give analytic mixed-state examples, study robustness under particle loss for different types of states and analyze purity–anticoherence trade-offs, including certified thresholds for low spins.
